WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.586
- And you know, you can't
always be number one.

00:00:02.586 --> 00:00:05.373
You can't always be, you
know, winning every award.

00:00:05.373 --> 00:00:07.335
But, I think everything
I ever went through,

00:00:07.335 --> 00:00:11.418
made me who I am, and
motivated me more, to be...

00:00:12.256 --> 00:00:13.639
To strive for what I wanted to do.

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:17.295
I want to be singing, 20 years
from now, 30 years from now.

00:00:17.295 --> 00:00:19.049
That's just always been my dream.

00:00:19.049 --> 00:00:20.560
- Hello, Believe Nation, it's Evan!

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.175
My one word is believe,

00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:23.621
and I believe in people,

00:00:23.621 --> 00:00:25.279
more than they believe in themselves,

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:26.436
and my sincere hope,

00:00:26.436 --> 00:00:29.802
is that if you see in
yourself, what I see in you,

00:00:29.802 --> 00:00:31.602
you'll be able to change the planet.

00:00:31.602 --> 00:00:32.877
So, to help you on your journey,

00:00:32.877 --> 00:00:35.002
today, we're going to learn
from singer/songwriter,

00:00:35.002 --> 00:00:38.997
Mariah Carey, and my take on
her top ten rules for success!

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:40.960
Rule number eight, is
my personal favorite,

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.549
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:43.549 --> 00:00:44.606
Also, as you're watching,

00:00:44.606 --> 00:00:46.809
if you hear something, that
really resonates with you,

00:00:46.809 --> 00:00:48.248
please leave it down
in the comments below,

00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:49.099
and put quotes around it,

00:00:49.099 --> 00:00:51.511
so other people can be
inspired by it as well.

00:00:51.511 --> 00:00:53.222
And if you leave it, within
the first couple hours,

00:00:53.222 --> 00:00:54.269
of this video going live,

00:00:54.269 --> 00:00:58.436
you have a chance, to win
one of two daily prizes.

00:01:00.261 --> 00:01:03.094
(inspiring music)

00:01:10.547 --> 00:01:13.244
- Your voice is one of the
more extraordinary things,

00:01:13.244 --> 00:01:15.417
in modern music.
- Thank you.

00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:18.102
- [Interviewer] When in your
life, did you first realize,

00:01:18.102 --> 00:01:20.739
there's something special,
that comes out of my throat?

00:01:20.739 --> 00:01:23.345
- The first time, for me,
that I actually felt it,

00:01:23.345 --> 00:01:24.573
was when I was six years old,

00:01:24.573 --> 00:01:27.610
and I was walkin' down the
street, with this little girl,

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.032
and we're singin' at the same time,

00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:30.002
and then she stopped singing,

00:01:30.002 --> 00:01:32.261
and she started listening to me.

00:01:32.261 --> 00:01:34.577
And then, I realized it, and I stopped.

00:01:34.577 --> 00:01:35.705
And I was like, what?

00:01:35.705 --> 00:01:36.954
And she goes, "When you sing,

00:01:36.954 --> 00:01:39.601
it sounds like there's music with you."

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:40.434
- Wow.

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:41.744
- [Mariah] I never forgot that.

00:01:41.744 --> 00:01:44.335
That was something, that
made me feel special,

00:01:44.335 --> 00:01:46.088
because I didn't feel special as a kid.

00:01:46.088 --> 00:01:48.271
I felt different, I felt weird.

00:01:48.271 --> 00:01:50.390
I didn't feel pretty, I
didn't feel like I fit in.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:52.494
I didn't feel a lot of things,

00:01:52.494 --> 00:01:55.498
that make kids develop their self-esteem.

00:01:55.498 --> 00:01:57.524
That moment stuck with me,

00:01:57.524 --> 00:02:00.844
and made me feel like, no
matter what, that's in here,

00:02:00.844 --> 00:02:03.119
and that's something,
that nobody can take.

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:07.467
Every time I hear one of
my songs on the radio,

00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:09.203
I sit there, and say,

00:02:09.203 --> 00:02:11.662
okay now, I should have
sang that line like this,

00:02:11.662 --> 00:02:13.099
and done that line like that,

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:15.583
or, oh that's rancid, or whatever,

00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:19.838
(chuckles) 'cause I'm just
never satisfied with my work.

00:02:19.838 --> 00:02:21.729
I always feel like I could've done,

00:02:21.729 --> 00:02:25.263
I could have done it just a
little bit differently, but...

00:02:25.263 --> 00:02:29.257
You know, I feel that, for a
first effort, I'm proud of it,

00:02:29.257 --> 00:02:32.191
and I'm just looking forward
to completing the writing

00:02:32.191 --> 00:02:34.743
that I'm doing for my
second album right now,

00:02:34.743 --> 00:02:36.498
and make the best album that I can,

00:02:36.498 --> 00:02:39.522
and just look ahead, and not look behind.

00:02:39.522 --> 00:02:40.797
- [Interviewer] You set this

00:02:40.797 --> 00:02:43.341
really substantial level of
success for yourself already.

00:02:43.341 --> 00:02:45.286
Do you feel like you
have to always, kind of,

00:02:45.286 --> 00:02:46.649
stay at that level?

00:02:46.649 --> 00:02:49.975
- I don't, because I've
been very, very fortunate,

00:02:49.975 --> 00:02:52.666
to have what's happened
me, have happened to me,

00:02:52.666 --> 00:02:56.014
and I mean, from now on,
all I really want to do,

00:02:56.014 --> 00:02:57.459
is continue to grow, as an artist,

00:02:57.459 --> 00:02:59.282
and to just continue to putting out music,

00:02:59.282 --> 00:03:03.410
and hope people, that have
supported me, will stay with me.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.614
And you can't always be number one.

00:03:05.614 --> 00:03:08.611
You can't always be winning every award.

00:03:08.611 --> 00:03:10.814
But, just to be putting music out there,

00:03:10.814 --> 00:03:13.887
and to be having some level of success,

00:03:13.887 --> 00:03:15.099
has always been my goal.

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:16.353
- What kind of student were you?

00:03:16.353 --> 00:03:18.698
I also read, that your
nickname was Mirage,

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:21.465
because you were a mirage, in class.

00:03:21.465 --> 00:03:22.687
(Mariah laughing)

00:03:22.687 --> 00:03:26.080
You were there sometimes,
most of the time, you weren't!

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:26.915
Is that true?

00:03:26.915 --> 00:03:30.112
- Well, I mean, I don't want
to encourage anybody in that,

00:03:30.112 --> 00:03:32.476
to go to school, or anything, you know.

00:03:32.476 --> 00:03:35.287
Far away from me, but, I liked school.

00:03:35.287 --> 00:03:38.265
I was more social in school,
than I was a student,

00:03:38.265 --> 00:03:41.714
and I always knew I was
going to be a singer.

00:03:41.714 --> 00:03:42.845
That's my thing.

00:03:42.845 --> 00:03:45.041
I'm very lucky that I
am, 'cause right now,

00:03:45.041 --> 00:03:46.602
who knows what I'd be doing.

00:03:46.602 --> 00:03:51.014
But, I don't know, I had trouble
getting up in the morning,

00:03:51.014 --> 00:03:52.070
that's why.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.845
I couldn't take, getting
to first period on time.

00:03:54.845 --> 00:03:56.811
But, I think, everything
I ever went through,

00:03:56.811 --> 00:03:59.663
made me who I am, and motivated me more,

00:03:59.663 --> 00:04:03.157
to be, to strive for what I wanted to do,

00:04:03.157 --> 00:04:05.137
because sometimes, if
you're too comfortable,

00:04:05.137 --> 00:04:05.970
when you're growing up,

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.899
it's just like, yeah, well,
I'll do this for a while,

00:04:07.899 --> 00:04:08.732
and then I'll do that,

00:04:08.732 --> 00:04:11.911
but I knew that I had to go for it.

00:04:11.911 --> 00:04:13.119
- [Meredith] You're
under a lot of scrutiny.

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:16.116
You've gone through a lot,
a lot in recent years,

00:04:16.116 --> 00:04:18.908
so you must feel particularly
happy about the success

00:04:18.908 --> 00:04:19.766
of this CD.

00:04:19.766 --> 00:04:20.811
It's not just selling, what was it,

00:04:20.811 --> 00:04:23.075
400,000 in the first week?
- Yeah.

00:04:23.075 --> 00:04:24.364
- [Meredith] I mean,
that's pretty incredible!

00:04:24.364 --> 00:04:27.939
(audience cheering)

00:04:27.939 --> 00:04:29.051
That's a big comeback, though!

00:04:29.051 --> 00:04:30.887
A lot of people are saying,
it was over for you.

00:04:30.887 --> 00:04:31.720
- Yeah.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:33.184
You know, a lot of people were very ready,

00:04:33.184 --> 00:04:34.306
to write me off completely,

00:04:34.306 --> 00:04:36.593
and it's like, you
know, I never write off,

00:04:36.593 --> 00:04:38.213
and I'm not tryin' to whatever,

00:04:38.213 --> 00:04:39.879
but there's certain people,
who I'm friends with,

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:41.695
who people have written off,

00:04:41.695 --> 00:04:44.020
and they've come back,
to have stellar years,

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:45.863
and I have never written them off.

00:04:45.863 --> 00:04:47.395
I mean, it was my big moment,

00:04:47.395 --> 00:04:49.636
and they were not having,
necessarily, such a huge moment.

00:04:49.636 --> 00:04:51.179
They were never written off, in my book,

00:04:51.179 --> 00:04:52.827
'cause you can't write off talent.

00:04:52.827 --> 00:04:54.423
So, it's like, when people do that,

00:04:54.423 --> 00:04:56.985
it's just not the right energy,
it's not the right thing,

00:04:56.985 --> 00:04:58.349
but yes it does feel good,

00:04:58.349 --> 00:05:00.376
and I have to thank my fans,
'cause they've stuck with me,

00:05:00.376 --> 00:05:02.897
and thank God, it's my
biggest first week, ever!

00:05:02.897 --> 00:05:04.453
You can't really fake it.

00:05:04.453 --> 00:05:06.320
You kind of have to love it, that much,

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.653
to make something, that lasts this long.

00:05:10.515 --> 00:05:14.788
People consider Fantasy a
pivotal, groundbreaking,

00:05:14.788 --> 00:05:18.455
kind of a moment, for
pop music, and for me.

00:05:19.763 --> 00:05:22.942
I had the idea, to use the
Tom Tom Club, Genius of Love,

00:05:22.942 --> 00:05:24.190
when I was at an amusement park,

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.982
and I was listening to the
radio, when it came on,

00:05:25.982 --> 00:05:28.776
and I was like, this'd be great.

00:05:28.776 --> 00:05:32.443
("Fantasy" by Mariah Carey)

00:05:38.034 --> 00:05:40.937
♫ When you walk by every night

00:05:40.937 --> 00:05:43.300
♫ Talkin' sweet and lookin' fine

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.981
♫ I get kind of hectic inside ♫

00:05:46.981 --> 00:05:48.875
There were different versions of the song,

00:05:48.875 --> 00:05:51.117
and I think most people have the memory

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:53.723
of the one, featuring ODB.

00:05:53.723 --> 00:05:54.577
It was a classic.

00:05:54.577 --> 00:05:55.840
Nobody expected it.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:59.807
♫ Me and Mariah go back
like babies with pacifiers

00:05:59.807 --> 00:06:01.821
♫ Old Dirt Dog no liar

00:06:01.821 --> 00:06:03.285
♫ Keep your fantasy hot like fire ♫

00:06:03.285 --> 00:06:04.118
- [Mariah] You know,

00:06:04.118 --> 00:06:05.202
it took a little bit of pushing,

00:06:05.202 --> 00:06:07.670
and pressure to record company,

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:11.629
because I don't think they
understood why I wanted to do it,

00:06:11.629 --> 00:06:13.583
and it's 'cause I wanted
to have a hot remix.

00:06:13.583 --> 00:06:15.823
♫ Ol' Dirty Bastard across the globe

00:06:15.823 --> 00:06:20.282
♫ I'm in Heaven ♫

00:06:20.282 --> 00:06:22.347
But, I was glad I got away with doing it,

00:06:22.347 --> 00:06:25.401
without the record company
listening to ODB's album,

00:06:25.401 --> 00:06:27.487
because it has certain topics on it,

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:31.091
that they might not have
approved of, for me.

00:06:31.091 --> 00:06:33.442
♫ Fantasy ♫

00:06:33.442 --> 00:06:36.627
(crowd cheering)

00:06:36.627 --> 00:06:38.765
- [Interviewer] Apart from the
Whitney Houston comparisons,

00:06:38.765 --> 00:06:41.282
and continuous surprise
over her vocal range...

00:06:41.282 --> 00:06:44.431
("Emotions" by Mariah Carey)

00:06:44.431 --> 00:06:45.682
Many were initially surprised,

00:06:45.682 --> 00:06:47.692
that her voice belonged to a white woman.

00:06:47.692 --> 00:06:48.525
- [Mariah Voiceover] I don't know,

00:06:48.525 --> 00:06:51.153
everybody likes to put
categories on music, and people,

00:06:51.153 --> 00:06:53.028
and this is black, this is white.

00:06:53.028 --> 00:06:56.406
You know, and I just really
don't think there's a need,

00:06:56.406 --> 00:06:57.630
to categorize everything.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.711
I mean, what I do is music,
and music should be universal.

00:07:00.711 --> 00:07:03.195
It shouldn't be about, this is
too black, this is too white,

00:07:03.195 --> 00:07:04.153
whatever.

00:07:04.153 --> 00:07:05.660
I think, everybody can relate to music,

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:08.005
and that's what it's all about.

00:07:08.005 --> 00:07:09.756
- You don't really get a lot of credit,

00:07:09.756 --> 00:07:13.923
for being a great writer, and
having 18 number one singles.

00:07:14.849 --> 00:07:17.636
- I wrote 17 of 'em, that's
the thing. (chuckles)

00:07:17.636 --> 00:07:18.977
But, people don't talk about it,

00:07:18.977 --> 00:07:21.583
because I think, if you're
a female, there's an issue,

00:07:21.583 --> 00:07:23.625
and if you're not strumming a guitar,

00:07:23.625 --> 00:07:25.964
or playing a freaking flute, or a fiddle,

00:07:25.964 --> 00:07:27.990
suddenly, you're less of a writer,

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:29.675
because that's not your process.

00:07:29.675 --> 00:07:31.903
People don't realize, like
when I first started out,

00:07:31.903 --> 00:07:34.624
I made sure, I hit a lot of high notes.

00:07:34.624 --> 00:07:38.032
♫ I had a vision of love

00:07:38.032 --> 00:07:41.211
♫ And it was all that you've given to me ♫

00:07:41.211 --> 00:07:44.095
If I just started out,
singing softly, only,

00:07:44.095 --> 00:07:46.770
people wouldn't necessarily
have taken notice.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.360
It was just about the voice.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.123
- Do you consider yourself a writer first,

00:07:51.123 --> 00:07:52.528
or a singer first?

00:07:52.528 --> 00:07:55.561
- I do sort of feel like
I'm a writer, first.

00:07:55.561 --> 00:07:57.508
Technically, I don't have write the song,

00:07:57.508 --> 00:07:58.341
in order to sing it.

00:07:58.341 --> 00:08:00.856
Somebody could write a
song, and present it to me.

00:08:00.856 --> 00:08:03.429
I don't feel fulfilled,
as an artist, that way.

00:08:03.429 --> 00:08:04.774
- [Interviewer] You said,
your parents divorced,

00:08:04.774 --> 00:08:05.908
when you were three.

00:08:05.908 --> 00:08:08.260
What was it like, not having
a father around the house?

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:10.752
Do you feel like you
missed out on anything?

00:08:10.752 --> 00:08:12.387
- (sighs) I don't, at all.

00:08:12.387 --> 00:08:15.070
I'm actually glad, that I grew up,

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:18.277
with just a single
parent, and a single mom,

00:08:18.277 --> 00:08:20.976
because I think it made me
more independent, you know?

00:08:20.976 --> 00:08:23.491
I mean, I'm not going
to sit here, and say,

00:08:23.491 --> 00:08:25.741
oh, I wish I had the Brady Bunch family.

00:08:25.741 --> 00:08:26.984
I used to, when I was little.

00:08:26.984 --> 00:08:28.979
I used to want to have the perfect family,

00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:30.153
and I used to be envious of people,

00:08:30.153 --> 00:08:32.018
that had the perfect,
picture perfect thing,

00:08:32.018 --> 00:08:33.578
but nothing's perfect.

00:08:33.578 --> 00:08:35.495
So, you know, I'm glad,

00:08:36.331 --> 00:08:37.631
about everything that I ever went through,

00:08:37.631 --> 00:08:40.301
and I think that my mom
is a major reason why

00:08:40.301 --> 00:08:41.531
I'm here today.

00:08:41.531 --> 00:08:42.616
I'm not trying to prove anything.

00:08:42.616 --> 00:08:45.029
I'm just really trying
to have a long career,

00:08:45.029 --> 00:08:46.339
as a singer/songwriter,

00:08:46.339 --> 00:08:50.413
and I want to be singing 20 years
from now, 30 years from now.

00:08:50.413 --> 00:08:51.754
That's just always been my dream,

00:08:51.754 --> 00:08:54.756
and it's incredible, to be
able to have the success,

00:08:54.756 --> 00:08:56.637
and I just want to continue going.

